---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, ORG_ID, REPO } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'One command for an atomic Linux desktop: re-color GNOME in one step, check the system’s health, set up a coding agent in a toolbox, update, and roll back.';

// The CLI's own usage text, verbatim from cli/pulsar. A transcript, not a
// retyping: if the help changes, this is the place that has to change with it.


import { USAGE } from '../../data/cli';
const usage = USAGE.join('\n');

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'The pulsar command',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/cli', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const SETUP = [
  'sudo pulsar setup apps         # install any default Flatpak that is missing',
  'pulsar setup devbox            # a dev distrobox, pulsar-dev, from /usr/share/pulsar/distrobox.ini',
  'sudo pulsar setup gamemode     # enroll accounts in the gamemode group',
  'pulsar setup quadlet           # the quadlet template, and container auto-update',
  'pulsar setup gamescale --platform steam   # let a Flatpak launcher use gamescale',
].join('\n');

const JSON_EXAMPLES = [
  "pulsar doctor --json | jq -r '.checks[] | select(.status != \"ok\") | .summary'",
  "pulsar update --check --json    # {status, booted, latest, image, staged}",
  "pulsar manifest --json | jq 'del(.host)'    # the manifest baked into the image",
].join('\n');

// where each command is explained properly
const WHERE: [string, string, string][] = [
  ['doctor, report', 'Troubleshooting', '/docs/troubleshooting'],
  ['status, update, rollback, pin, checkpoint', 'Updates and rollback', '/docs/updates'],
  ['theme', 'Themes', '/docs/themes'],
  ['agent, mcp', 'Coding agents', '/docs/agents'],
  ['setup', 'Where things go', '/docs/where-things-go'],
  ['manifest, changelog, sbom, verify', 'Provenance', '/docs/provenance'],
];

const TOC = [
  { id: 'usage', label: 'Everything it does', level: 2 },
  { id: 'json', label: 'For scripts', level: 2 },
  { id: 'setup', label: 'setup', level: 2 },
  { id: 'doctor', label: 'Why doctor exists', level: 2 },
  { id: 'update', label: 'update, not upgrade', level: 2 },
  { id: 'name', label: 'Another pulsar?', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="The pulsar command"
  fullTitle="The pulsar command — themes, health, agents and rollback"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/cli"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>The pulsar command</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    One command for everything you do with an atomic system: make it look like yours, check it’s
    healthy, see what changed, hand it to an agent, and undo it if you need to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="usage">Everything it does</h2>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar --help" code={usage}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    Plain <code>pulsar</code> runs <code>doctor</code>. <code>pulsar help &lt;command&gt;</code>, or{' '}
    <code>--help</code> after any command, gives that command’s own usage. Only the commands marked
    root ask for it; everything that just reads works as you.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Commands</th><th>Explained in</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {WHERE.map(([cmds, label, href]) => (
        <tr><td><code>{cmds}</code></td><td><a href={href}>{label}</a></td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="json">For scripts</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--json</code> works anywhere on the line. A command that has no JSON output says so and
    exits with an error, instead of handing your script prose it will choke on. Exit codes carry
    answers too: <code>doctor</code> exits 1 only on a failed check, and{' '}
    <code>update --check</code> exits 10 when an update is out.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="json" code={JSON_EXAMPLES}></arc-code-block>

  <h2 id="setup">setup: what an image cannot bake</h2>
  <p>
    Some state belongs to your machine or your account, not to the image: which apps you have, which
    groups you are in, your containers. Each <code>setup</code> recipe does one of those, and says
    so if you run it with or without <code>sudo</code> when it should not be.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="setup" code={SETUP}></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    When to use a toolbox, a distrobox, a quadlet or neither:{' '}
    <a href="/docs/where-things-go">Where things go</a>. Why gamescale needs its step:{' '}
    <a href="/docs/gamescale#flatpak">gamescale</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="doctor">Why doctor exists</h2>
  <p>
    <code>systemctl is-active scx.service</code> reported active for minutes at a stretch while no
    scheduler was attached and the machine ran stock EEVDF. Unit state is not system state. So{' '}
    <code>doctor</code> reads <code>/sys/kernel/sched_ext/state</code> and the other places where
    the truth actually lives, and <code>--json</code> makes it scriptable. Only a <em>fail</em> sets
    a nonzero exit; a scheduler that did not attach merely warns, because the machine is entirely
    usable without one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reads go through <code>rpm-ostree</code>, which works unprivileged; only the commands that
    change the system ask for root. <code>bootc status</code> needs root even to read, and a health
    check you need sudo for is one you will not run. <code>pulsar sbom</code> is generated, never
    baked: a file inside the image cannot describe the image containing it, so it reads the live rpm
    database, through the same script the nightly uses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="update">update, not upgrade</h2>
  <p>
    Use <code>pulsar update</code> rather than <code>bootc upgrade</code> directly if you have
    layered anything with <code>rpm-ostree install</code>. bootc’s model is the container image
    alone: it does not know about your layer and will not carry it forward.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar update</code> checks the booted deployment first and hands off to{' '}
    <code>rpm-ostree upgrade</code> when there is something to preserve; the <code>updates</code>{' '}
    line of <code>pulsar doctor</code> tells you which side of that line you are on.{' '}
    <code>pulsar update --check</code> answers “is there a newer image” without root.{' '}
    <a href="/docs/updates">The whole update story</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="name">Another pulsar?</h2>
  <p>
    The Pulsar code editor also ships a command called <code>pulsar</code>. As a Flatpak it
    collides with nothing, but its RPM or AppImage can land earlier on your <code>PATH</code>, and
    then <code>pulsar doctor</code> quietly opens an editor. <code>/usr/bin/pulsar</code> always
    reaches this one, and doctor’s <code>cli</code> check tells you when something is shadowing it.
  </p>
  <p class="hint">The source is <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/cli/pulsar`}>cli/pulsar</a>, one shell script.</p>
</Docs>
